          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Let ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "y=\\dfrac{2x+1}{3x+1}",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ".",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "p38-cc-1-3-instruction",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 2,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "What error was made in the following calculations?",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p38-cc-1-3-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$y'=\\dfrac{2}{3}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The numerator and denominator were differentiated separately.",
          "worked": "The quotient rule must be applied to the whole fraction: $y'=\\dfrac{2(3x+1)-3(2x+1)}{(3x+1)^2}=\\dfrac{-1}{(3x+1)^2}$. Differentiating the top and bottom separately does not give the derivative of their quotient.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "p38-cc-1-3-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$y'=\\dfrac{2(3x+1)+3(2x+1)}{(3x+1)^2}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The sign between the numerator terms should be minus.",
          "worked": "The quotient rule gives $y'=\\dfrac{u'v-uv'}{v^2}$. The numerator must therefore be $2(3x+1)-3(2x+1)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 22
      },
      {
        "id": "p38-cc-1-3-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$y'=\\dfrac{3(2x+1)-2(3x+1)}{(3x+1)^2}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The numerator terms are in the wrong order.",
          "worked": "With $u=2x+1$ and $v=3x+1$, the numerator is $u'v-uv'=2(3x+1)-3(2x+1)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 12
      }
    ]
  },
